\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The dark matter problem as a structural correction, shown for a $10^{11} M_\odot $ galaxy. Each panel corresponds to one consequence of the correction factor $\mu (x) = x/(1+x)$ derived above. \textbf  {Top left:} Rotation curves (Newton declining in red, topology in blue, deep-MOND asymptote in green). \textbf  {Top right:} The correction factor itself, showing the enhanced (red) and Newtonian (blue) regimes. \textbf  {Bottom left:} How gravity weakens with distance: the transition from $1/r^2$ to $1/r$ on logarithmic axes. \textbf  {Bottom right:} Is mass really missing? The grey curve shows the mass unaccounted for at each radius using Newtonian gravity ($1/r^2$). The horizontal line marks the cited 85\% dark matter fraction. The blue line shows the corrected gravity ($1/r^2 \to 1/r$): no mass is unaccounted for. See text for detailed descriptions.}}{69}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:darkmatter}{{6}{69}{The dark matter problem as a structural correction, shown for a $10^{11} M_\odot $ galaxy. Each panel corresponds to one consequence of the correction factor $\mu (x) = x/(1+x)$ derived above. \textbf {Top left:} Rotation curves (Newton declining in red, topology in blue, deep-MOND asymptote in green). \textbf {Top right:} The correction factor itself, showing the enhanced (red) and Newtonian (blue) regimes. \textbf {Bottom left:} How gravity weakens with distance: the transition from $1/r^2$ to $1/r$ on logarithmic axes. \textbf {Bottom right:} Is mass really missing? The grey curve shows the mass unaccounted for at each radius using Newtonian gravity ($1/r^2$). The horizontal line marks the cited 85\% dark matter fraction. The blue line shows the corrected gravity ($1/r^2 \to 1/r$): no mass is unaccounted for. See text for detailed descriptions}{figure.6}{}}
